.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt ESPI_FLASH 4D
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm espi_flash
.Nd eSPI flash access channel driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /devices/ Ns Ar fch Ns Pa @0,0/espi@ Ns Ar n Ns Pa /espi_flash@3:flash
.Pp
.In sys/espi_flash.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides access to a flash device that is attached to an eSPI target
and reached over the flash access channel of an eSPI controller.
It attaches as a child of
.Xr espi 4D
when the controller's target has flash attached to it and the channel has been
enabled.
.Pp
The device is a character special file which only a privileged process in the
global zone may open.
The
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
families of functions, which include
.Xr pread 2 ,
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
.Xr readv 2
and
.Xr writev 2 ,
transfer data at the file offset, which is the address within the flash.
Transfers of any size and alignment are accepted.
The driver divides each into the requests that the eSPI link allows, so a
single call may result in many requests to the target.
If one of those fails after others have succeeded, the call returns the number
of bytes that were transferred.
The error is reported only if nothing was transferred, so a caller that repeats
the call for the remainder will see it then if it persists.
Non-blocking I/O is not supported.
.Pp
The driver does not erase the flash before writing to it.
As is usual for NOR flash, the blocks to be written must first be erased.
With this driver that is done with the
.Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
ioctl.
The data should be read back afterwards to verify it.
The target may not allow writes or erases, for example until the service
processor permits them, in which case the operation fails with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
The size of the flash is not reported by eSPI.
When the platform supplies it, reads stop at the end of the flash, writes that
start at or beyond it fail with
.Er ENOSPC ,
and the size is what
.Xr stat 2
reports for the device while it is open.
When the size is not known the whole 32-bit eSPI flash address space is
accessible.
What then happens to a request for an address that the target does not
implement is up to the target, which will usually fail it, resulting in
.Er EIO .
.Pp
.Xr fwflash 8
can be used to save the contents of the flash to a file and to program it.
.Pp
The device may be opened with
.Dv O_EXCL
to prevent other opens while the flash is being reprogrammed.
.Sh PROPERTIES
The driver records the following properties on its node.
They are informational and cannot be set.
The
.Pa /usr/platform/oxide/bin/espi
utility displays them with its
.Cm status
subcommand.
.Bl -tag -width Sy
.It Sy Size
The size of the flash in bytes.
This is a property of the minor node and is absent when the size is not known.
.It Sy flash-payload , flash-max-read
The largest number of bytes in a single flash write request and a single
flash read request on the eSPI link.
.It Sy flash-erase-sizes
The erase block sizes that the target advertised, with bit
.Em n
set for 2^n KiB blocks.
.El
.Sh IOCTLS
The ioctls are defined in
.In sys/espi_flash.h .
.Bl -tag -width Dv
.It Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_INFO
Describes the device.
The argument is a pointer to an
.Vt espi_flash_info_t
which is filled in by the driver:
.Bd -literal
typedef struct espi_flash_info {
	uint64_t	efi_size;
	uint32_t	efi_payload;
	uint32_t	efi_maxread;
	uint32_t	efi_erase_sizes;
	uint32_t	efi_pad;
} espi_flash_info_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa efi_size
is the size of the flash in bytes, or 0 if it is not known.
.Fa efi_payload
and
.Fa efi_maxread
are the largest number of bytes that a single write request and a single read
request on the eSPI link may carry.
A read or write is divided into requests of at most these sizes, and so that
no request crosses an address that is a multiple of the size.
A transfer that is not aligned to the size therefore begins or ends with a
shorter request, and transfers that are multiples of these sizes and aligned to
them need the fewest requests.
.Fa efi_erase_sizes
has bit
.Em n
set if the target has advertised that blocks of 2^n KiB can be erased.
If it is 0 the target advertised no erase block sizes and the driver accepts
any that the eSPI protocol can request.
.It Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
Erases one block of the flash.
The file descriptor must be open for writing.
The argument is a pointer to an
.Vt espi_flash_erase_t :
.Bd -literal
typedef struct espi_flash_erase {
	uint64_t	efe_offset;
	uint64_t	efe_length;
} espi_flash_erase_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa efe_length
is the size of the block and must be one of 4 KiB, 32 KiB, 64 KiB or 128 KiB,
which are the sizes that the eSPI protocol can request, and, if the target
advertised erase block sizes, one of those.
.Fa efe_offset
is the address of the block and must be a multiple of
.Fa efe_length .
.El
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
The
.Nm
driver is only available on the oxide platform.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Pa
.It Pa /platform/oxide/kernel/drv/amd64/espi_flash
64-bit AMD64 ELF kernel driver
.El
.Sh ERRORS
When a call fails,
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
The more common errors are listed below for each kind of call.
.Ss open
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBUSY
The device is already open and
.Dv O_EXCL
was requested, or it is open with
.Dv O_EXCL .
.It Er EINVAL
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
or
.Dv O_NDELAY
was requested.
.It Er ENXIO
The device does not exist.
.It Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged or is not in the global zone.
.El
.Ss read and write
These errors, other than those that concern the data, can also be returned by
.Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE .
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBADF
The file descriptor is not open for reading, for a read, or for writing, for a
write.
.It Er EBUSY
The eSPI controller could not accept a request.
Either another user of the controller, such as system firmware, did not release
it in time, or the target did not become ready for another flash request.
.It Er EFAULT
The driver could not copy data to or from the address space of the calling
process.
.It Er EINTR
A signal was received while waiting for another operation on the flash to
finish.
.It Er EINVAL
The file offset is negative.
.It Er EIO
The target reported that it could not complete a request, or the data it
returned did not match what was asked for.
.It Er ENOSPC
A write starts at or beyond the end of the flash.
.It Er ENOTSUP
The flash access channel is not enabled.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
The target did not complete a request in time.
.El
.Ss ioctl
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBADF
.Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
was requested on a file descriptor that is not open for writing.
.It Er EFAULT
The driver could not copy data to or from the address space of the calling
process.
.It Er EINVAL
The erase block size is not one that can be requested, the block is not
aligned to its size, or it lies outside the flash.
.It Er ENOTSUP
The erase block size is not one that the target advertised.
.It Er ENOTTY
The request is not one that the driver implements.
.El
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Uncommitted
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr pread 2 ,
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr readv 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr writev 2 ,
.Xr espi 4D ,
.Xr fwflash 8
